```

The reload protocol should be rigid:

1. Load `migration/STATE.json`.
2. Load the phase-specific artifacts referenced in `artifacts`.
3. Recompute a small fingerprint of the current repo slice: current `HEAD`, build-root files, relevant `pom.xml` or `settings.gradle` or `build.gradle`, and changed paths.
4. If any fingerprint differs from the stored baseline and the difference is unexplained, emit `DRIFT_DETECTED`, append it to `FAILURE_INDEX.md`, and switch `status` to `BLOCKED`.
5. Only then proceed to the next step.

This design is especially important for monorepos and multi-module projects, because Maven’s reactor model and Gradle’s multi-project or composite build behavior mean that “the changed files” and “the effective build graph” are not the same thing. citeturn0search2turn0search3turn0search7turn0search10

The phase flow should look like this:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Readiness] --> B[Discovery]
    B --> C[Service Definition]
    C --> D[Target Architecture]
    D --> E[Migration Plan]
    E --> F{Approval Gate}
    F -- plan only --> Z[Stop]
    F -- approve same-language --> G[Implementation]
    F -- approve cross-language --> G
    G --> H[Feedback Loop]
    H --> I{Pass}
    I -- no --> J[Failure Recovery]
    J --> H
    I -- yes --> K[Cutover Readiness]
    K --> L{Cutover Gate}
    L -- no --> Z
    L -- yes --> M[Controlled Cutover]
```

The approval gate is the control break between “read-only planning” and “code-changing execution.” Current custom-agent support explicitly fits that model: a planning agent can be restricted to read-only tools and hand off to an implementation agent only after the user reviews the output. citeturn11view0

## Agents and skills

The recommended role set is shown below. The agents are prompt-layer personas; the skills are reusable capabilities. Current Copilot docs explicitly distinguish these concepts: use **custom agents** for persistent role behavior and tool restrictions, **prompt files** for lightweight single-task commands, and **skills** for portable capabilities and optional resources. citeturn11view0turn9view0turn5view3

### Required agents

| Agent | Primary responsibility | Tool posture | Must emit | Must stop when |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `discovery` | Inventory repo, build roots, modules, entry points, config, runtime semantics | Read-only | `FACTS.md`, `MODULE_GRAPH.md`, `OWNERSHIP_MAP.md`, `STATE.json` | build topology is ambiguous |
| `service-boundary` | Infer bounded contexts and service candidates | Read-only | `SERVICE_CATALOG.md`, `BOUNDARY_DECISIONS.md` | ownership is ambiguous or service has no stable API/data boundary |
| `architecture` | Define target topology, contracts, data ownership, sync/async paths | Read-only | `API_BASELINE.md`, `INTERACTION_MATRIX.md`, `DB_PLAN.md` | transactional or security semantics are unresolved |
| `planner` | Produce waves, rollback plan, risk register, test plan, mutation plan | Read-only | `EXECUTION_PLAN.md`, `RISKS.md`, `APPROVAL_GATE.md` | approvals or unspecified target constraints are missing |
| `implementation` | Apply one patch batch at a time | Edit-capable | patch manifest, code changes, `PATCH_LOG.ndjson` | next patch would cross an unverified boundary |
| `stabilization` | Recover build, test, config, or wiring regressions | Edit-capable | `FAILURE_INDEX.md`, updated logs | fix count exceeds policy or root cause remains unknown |
| `mutation` | Run PIT policy and improve test strength | Read-only or limited edit | `MUTATION_HUNTER.md`, mutation findings | critical mutants survive without a remediation plan |
| `cutover` | Validate readiness and propose route/ownership transfer | Read-only | `CUTOVER_CHECKLIST.md`, final approval payload | any cutover criterion is unmet |

### Required skills

| Skill | Inputs | Outputs | Core heuristics / algorithm | Failure modes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `repo-scan` | filesystem slice, root descriptors | language/runtime/build inventory | prioritize `pom.xml`, `settings.gradle`, `build.gradle`, `src/main`, `src/test`, config files; classify by evidence only | nested repos, vendored code, generated sources mistaken as authored code |
| `build-graph` | Maven or Gradle files | build roots, module DAG, plugin/BOM map | parse Maven aggregation/inheritance, Gradle subprojects and included builds; record parent/child/BOM/plugin inheritance separately | mixed Maven and Gradle, ad hoc shell builds, missing parent POM |
| `runtime-semantics` | Spring annotations, config, entry points | transaction/security/async/scheduling map | detect `@Transactional`, `@TransactionalEventListener`, `@Async`, `@Scheduled`, servlet filters, Actuator usage, external config | proxy boundaries not obvious, reactive and imperative code mixed |
| `ownership-map` | packages, module names, events, repos, docs | domain ownership candidates | favor package cohesion, aggregate roots, repo-to-service affinity, module API vs internal access | shared “common/core/platform” modules hide business logic |
| `service-candidate` | ownership map, build graph, runtime semantics | service candidates with exclusions | reject nano-services, purely technical slices, or boundaries lacking data or logic autonomy | ambiguous ownership, chatty dependencies, distributed transaction risk |
| `api-freeze` | controllers, listeners, clients, OpenAPI or contracts if present | API and event baselines | freeze request/response shapes, headers, auth assumptions, event schemas; mark unspecified when absent | undocumented endpoints, implicit headers, schema drift |
| `data-cutover` | schema inventory, event flows, DB ownership map | migration mode and rollback plan | classify per table: keep, split, replicate, outbox, CDC, backfill; prefer expand/contract | cross-domain foreign keys, irreversible transforms, unknown data volume |
| `test-strength` | unit/integration/contract tests, changed scope | targeted test plan and risk score | prefer characterization tests for unclear behavior; use Spring test slices, Testcontainers, Pact where applicable | no tests, flaky tests, unsafe reliance on H2 or mocks |
| `pit-gate` | changed classes, critical logic map | mutation score, surviving mutant list | run PIT only on critical or changed logic; require higher thresholds for state transitions | equivalent mutants, excessive runtime, unreproducible test selection |
| `rollback` | patch manifest, config changes, DB plan | revert steps and verification | every patch must declare revert operation and proof path; no irreversible step without explicit gate | destructive DB DDL, no baseline snapshot, route switch without fallback |

A useful optional accelerator is **Spring Modulith**. Where the monolith already has coherent packages, the discovery agent can use it as an evidence source because `ApplicationModules.verify()` checks for cycles and internal-package violations, and the `Documenter` can generate module diagrams and canvases including beans, events, and configuration properties. This is not a replacement for migration planning, but it is a strong discovery and verification aid. citeturn12view1turn12view2turn6search5

### Role interaction model

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    D[Discovery Agent] --> SB[Service Boundary Agent]
    SB --> A[Architecture Agent]
    A --> P[Planner Agent]
    P -->|approval required| I[Implementation Agent]
    I --> S[Stabilization Agent]
    I --> M[Mutation Agent]
    S --> M
    M --> C[Cutover Agent]
    C -->|if not ready| P
```

### Skill invocation syntax

Native Copilot skill loading is implicit: the platform decides when to use a skill based on the prompt and the skill description, and when it does so the skill context is injected for the session. For production auditability, the prompt-only migration design should add a **deterministic prompt convention** on top of that: every agent must echo the invoked skills in both prose and JSON using a syntax like `[[SKILL:build-graph]]` and `invoked_skills: ["build-graph"]`. That syntax is **not** a native GitHub feature; it is a prompt-layer convention recommended here to make transcripts auditable and diffable. citeturn5view3turn5view2

## Execution plan and safety model

The execution plan should be phase-based and small-batch. Same-language and cross-language should share the same early phases and diverge only after the approval gate.

### Phase plan

| Phase | Active agent | Required outputs | Gate to continue |
|---|---|---|---|
| Readiness | `discovery` | repo type, build roots, language inventory, open blockers | build topology known |
| Discovery | `discovery` | facts, module graph, ownership candidates, runtime semantics | semantic blockers resolved or marked |
| Service definition | `service-boundary` | service catalog, exclusions, risk notes | each candidate has owner, API shape, data story |
| Target architecture | `architecture` | contracts, interactions, event model, observability model, DB strategy | no unresolved transactional ambiguity on chosen wave |
| Migration plan | `planner` | waves, scope per wave, rollback, validation, mutation plan | explicit user approval |
| Implementation | `implementation` | one patch batch only | patch feedback loop passes |
| Stabilization | `stabilization` | repaired build/tests/config/wiring | no open critical failures |
| Cutover readiness | `cutover` | acceptance report and route switch proposal | all cutover criteria pass |

### Feedback-loop enforcement

The feedback loop must be mandatory after **every** patch batch. Spring Boot’s test support, Spring Security’s test support, Actuator health endpoints, Micrometer integration, and PIT all support the individual checks needed for a credible gate; the prompt-only design here combines them into one deterministic sequence. citeturn0search1turn8search1turn1search3turn1search7turn3search6turn1search0

| Check | Minimum evidence | Fail action |
|---|---|---|
| Static | compile-inferrable imports, lint output, module sanity | stop patch; open `BUILD_FAILURE` or `STATIC_FAILURE` |
| Build | reactor or target subproject build succeeds | revert patch or isolate changed build root |
| Tests | targeted unit plus slice/integration tests for affected scope | add or repair tests before proceeding |
| Mutation | PIT threshold met for critical logic | remain blocked; improve tests |
| Functional equivalence | baseline behavior and side effects still match | revert or switch to characterization-first |
| Observability | health, metrics, trace correlation, log fields preserved | block cutover; fix instrumentation |
| Rollback | revert path documented and validated | mark patch invalid |

The test harness should be layered. Targeted Spring tests should be preferred first, then realistic dependency-backed tests with Testcontainers, then contract tests where service boundaries emerge, then mutation tests where business logic is critical. Pact supports Spring provider verification; Testcontainers supports JUnit 5 integration; Spring Boot supports application and slice testing; Spring Security supports security-aware test primitives. citeturn0search5turn3search0turn3search8turn3search1turn3search5turn8search1

### Cross-language safety rules

Cross-language migration is a separate operating mode, not a variant of same-language execution. The recommended policy is:

1. Default `LANGUAGE_MODE = SAME_LANGUAGE`.
2. Allow `CROSS_LANGUAGE` only with an explicit approval token and a specified target language and framework.
3. Freeze API and event contracts before any rewrite.
4. Capture characterization tests and golden payloads from the Java/Spring source.
5. Route side-by-side through a strangler facade or controlled shadow path.
6. Compare not just HTTP responses but DB side effects, emitted events, logs, metrics, auth behavior, and timeout/retry semantics.
7. Keep rollback immediate and route-based until equivalence is demonstrated across representative workloads.

That policy is grounded in two facts. First, the strangler-fig pattern reduces risk by incrementally replacing behavior behind a facade rather than doing a cut-over rewrite. Second, Spring runtime semantics are framework-specific: `@Transactional` is proxy-driven and thread-bound, transaction-bound events default to the commit phase, `@Async` and `@Scheduled` introduce separate execution paths, and Spring Security relies on an ordered filter chain. Those are exactly the kinds of semantics most likely to be lost in a naive translation to another stack. citeturn1search2turn1search10turn0search4turn0search12turn7search1turn3search3turn8search0

A practical equivalence checklist for cross-language waves is:

| Category | Must match before cutover |
|---|---|
| Contract | endpoint paths, verbs, status codes, auth rules, headers, idempotency behavior |
| Data | writes, read models, transaction visibility, event payloads, backfill correctness |
| Security | authentication entry points, authorization outcomes, security headers, failure responses |
| Async | retry semantics, ordering assumptions, scheduled jobs, timeout behavior |
| Observability | health, business metrics, trace IDs, alerting signals, structured logs |
| Operations | startup profile/config resolution, secrets source, rollback path, runbook parity |

### Data migration and database strategies

For database changes, the migration agent should prefer **expand/contract** over destructive changes, use versioned migrations through Flyway or Liquibase, and treat CDC/outbox as the preferred mechanism for asynchronous propagation when one service must publish changes that another consumes. Debezium’s outbox event router documents the purpose of safely and reliably exchanging data through an outbox table; Flyway documents repeatable, version-controlled migrations; and Liquibase documents rollback commands. The specific choice between dual-write, outbox, or CDC is a design decision, but the safest production default is to avoid direct dual-write unless there is an explicit approval and a verified fallback. citeturn2search0turn2search12turn2search1turn2search2turn2search10

| Strategy | Use when | Required proof | Rollback posture |
|---|---|---|---|
| Expand / contract schema | same DB, gradual ownership move | old and new code both run during overlap | high |
| Backfill | new service needs historical state | idempotent batch plan, progress markers, validation queries | medium to high |
| CDC / outbox | async propagation or read model sync | durable event schema, replay policy, lag monitoring | high |
| Dual-write | only when unavoidable and explicitly approved | exact write-order contract, reconciliation job, alerting, rollback switch | low by default |

Config and secrets should be treated as evidence-only inputs. Spring Boot’s configuration model is explicitly externalized across properties, YAML, env vars, and command-line arguments. If the repo does not expose a real secret source, the migration agent must mark the value `UNSPECIFIED` and block any claim of production readiness. It should never invent credentials, endpoints, or secret-manager bindings. citeturn2search3turn2search11

### Mutation-hunter policy

PIT exists because green tests are not always strong tests. Its official model is mutation of compiled bytecode using configurable mutators; the migration design should use that capability as a gate for critical logic, especially validations, state transitions, authorization checks, calculations, idempotency logic, and repository predicates. citeturn1search16turn1search12turn1search4

Recommended mutation thresholds for a prompt-only migration policy:

| Scope | Threshold | Policy |
|---|---|---|
| Critical migrated logic | `>= 85%` | required to leave stabilization |
| High-risk modules | `>= 75%` | required before route shift |
| Ordinary migrated modules | `>= 65%` | required before wave completion |
| Untouched legacy areas | informational only | never used to justify migration |

These thresholds are not vendor defaults; they are a conservative migration policy intended to make “green but weak” suites visible.

