### Audit artifacts and acceptance criteria

The artifact set below is the minimum needed for full auditability.

| Artifact | Produced by | Purpose | Acceptance criterion |
|---|---|---|---|
| `STATE.json` | every agent | canonical run state | valid JSON and current phase/step consistent |
| `FACTS.md` | `discovery` | evidence-backed facts only | every fact has source path or build evidence |
| `ASSUMPTIONS.md` | any agent | unresolved assumptions | each assumption has risk and owner |
| `MODULE_GRAPH.md` | `discovery` | build/module topology | root, child, and dependency distinctions are explicit |
| `SERVICE_CATALOG.md` | `service-boundary` | selected and rejected service candidates | each candidate has owner, contract story, and data story |
| `API_BASELINE.md` | `architecture` | endpoint and event baseline | all moved paths have explicit baseline or are marked unspecified |
| `DB_PLAN.md` | `architecture` | ownership, schema, backfill, CDC, rollback | no destructive action lacks approval and rollback |
| `EXECUTION_PLAN.md` | `planner` | wave sequencing and patch sizing | every wave has prerequisites, checks, rollback |
| `PATCH_LOG.ndjson` | `implementation` | immutable patch record | one entry per patch batch |
| `FEEDBACK_LOG.ndjson` | `stabilization` | immutable check outcomes | one entry per patch batch with pass/fail by check |
| `MUTATION_HUNTER.md` | `mutation` | mutation scope and evidence | critical paths have explicit decision |
| `FAILURE_INDEX.md` | `stabilization` | normalized failures and attempts | stalled or oscillating paths are visible |
| `CUTOVER_CHECKLIST.md` | `cutover` | final readiness record | every required gate is pass or explicitly blocked |

Spring Modulith can optionally generate documentation artifacts that fit naturally into this audit model, especially module diagrams and canvases. OpenRewrite also fits naturally, but only as a **skill-level helper** for code transformations or framework upgrades, because its official operating model is recipe-driven refactoring, not architectural decomposition or gated migration control. citeturn12view2turn12view0turn1search1

## Limitations and assumptions

A prompt-only system is still limited by context size, repository size, and tool access. Custom agents help by restricting tools and enabling handoffs, but they do not create a true transactionally isolated workflow engine. Prompt files are useful for reusable commands, but they remain an invocation layer, not a scheduler. Skills improve repeatability, but they are still instructions injected into a model context, not hard guarantees of execution. Some current Copilot customization features are also documented as preview or subject to change. citeturn11view0turn9view0turn5view2turn11view1

A prompt-only system also cannot guarantee semantic equivalence in the absence of executable evidence. That matters most for cross-language moves: a translated service can preserve endpoint shape and still break transaction timing, event ordering, security responses, or config resolution. For that reason, the system described here treats **same-language migration as the default** and **cross-language migration as an exceptional, explicitly approved mode**. That is an engineering judgment supported by the Spring runtime behaviors documented in the framework references. citeturn0search4turn7search1turn3search3turn8search0

The design above assumes the following are **unspecified unless observed or supplied**: target language, target framework, deployment platform, message broker, database engine, secret source, SLOs, cutover window, and regulatory constraints. Under this model, “unspecified” is a valid and expected state. It must never be silently filled with invention. The migration agent should preserve that label until evidence exists or the user approves a specific value. That is the core anti-hallucination rule of the whole system.
